      "text": "We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n|n + 6| = 2 - n\n\\]\nThe absolute value function \\( |x| \\) is defined as:\n\\[\n|x| = \n\\begin{cases} \nx & \\text{if } x \\geq 0 \\\\\n-x & \\text{if } x < 0 \n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nApplying this to our equation, we consider two cases: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\) and \\( n + 6 < 0 \\).\n\n**Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)**\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = n + 6 \\), so the equation becomes:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nSolving for \\( n \\), we add \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\nSubtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\nDivide by 2:\n\\[\nn = -2\n\\]\nWe need to check if this solution satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\):\n\\[\n-2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0\n\\]\nSince the condition is satisfied, \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution for this case.\n\n**Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)**\nIn this case, \\( |n + 6| = -(n + 6) = -n - 6 \\), so the equation becomes:\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nSolving for \\( n \\), we add \\( n \\) to both sides:\n\\[\n-6 = 2\n\\]\nThis is a contradiction, so there is no solution in this case.\n\nTherefore, the only solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
