      "text": "To solve the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The absolute value \\( |n + 6| \\) can be split into two cases based on the expression inside the absolute value.\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\)\nWhen \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\), the absolute value \\( |n + 6| \\) simplifies to \\( n + 6 \\). Therefore, the equation becomes:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nTo solve for \\( n \\), we add \\( n \\) to both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nn + n + 6 = 2\n\\]\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2\n\\]\nNext, we subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[\n2n = 2 - 6\n\\]\n\\[\n2n = -4\n\\]\nFinally, we divide both sides by 2:\n\\[\nn = -2\n\\]\nWe need to check if this solution satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 \\geq 0 \\). Substituting \\( n = -2 \\) into the condition, we get:\n\\[\n-2 + 6 = 4 \\geq 0\n\\]\nSince the condition is satisfied, \\( n = -2 \\) is a valid solution for this case.\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 6 < 0 \\)\nWhen \\( n + 6 < 0 \\), the absolute value \\( |n + 6| \\) simplifies to \\( -(n + 6) \\). Therefore, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n-(n + 6) = 2 - n\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n\n\\]\nTo solve for \\( n \\), we add \\( n \\) to both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n-n - 6 + n = 2 - n + n\n\\]\n\\[\n-6 = 2\n\\]\nThis is a contradiction, which means there is no value of \\( n \\) that satisfies the condition \\( n + 6 < 0 \\). Therefore, there are no solutions for this case.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe only solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is \\( n = -2 \\). Thus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
