      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The absolute value equation \\( |x| = a \\) implies two possible cases:\n1. \\( x = a \\)\n2. \\( x = -a \\)\n\nIn our case, \\( x = n + 6 \\) and \\( a = 2 - n \\). Therefore, the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) can be split into two cases:\n1. \\( n + 6 = 2 - n \\)\n2. \\( n + 6 = -(2 - n) \\)\n\nLet's solve each case separately.\n\n**Case 1: \\( n + 6 = 2 - n \\)**\n\nTo isolate \\( n \\), we first add \\( n \\) to both sides of the equation:\n\\[ n + n + 6 = 2 - n + n \\]\n\\[ 2n + 6 = 2 \\]\n\nNext, we subtract 6 from both sides:\n\\[ 2n + 6 - 6 = 2 - 6 \\]\n\\[ 2n = -4 \\]\n\nThen, we divide both sides by 2:\n\\[ n = \\frac{-4}{2} \\]\n\\[ n = -2 \\]\n\n**Case 2: \\( n + 6 = -(2 - n) \\)**\n\nFirst, simplify the right side of the equation:\n\\[ n + 6 = -2 + n \\]\n\nNext, we subtract \\( n \\) from both sides:\n\\[ n + 6 - n = -2 + n - n \\]\n\\[ 6 = -2 \\]\n\nThis is a contradiction, which means there is no solution from this case.\n\nTherefore, the only solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
